2 CONT
2 CONT Trotzig, Peter,^" was bom in Sweden, but he moved to Amsterdam  and became a merchant there. He was employed by the Swedish govern-  ment in Amsterdam in 1642 to take the place of Blommaert, and con-  tinued to serve his country f
2 CONC or a great many years, being made a com-  missary in 1 66 1. He hired Dutch sailors, officers and skilled laborers for  Swedish service, he bought ships and caused others to be built and he was  employed in various commercial tran
2 CONC sactions. He was the factor of the  New Sweden Company in Holland and purchased many of the cargoes  sent to the Delaware. About 1666 he returned to his native land and  was employed in the city government at Stockholm. He was kni
2 CONC ghted  and changed his name to Trotzenfelt. (The author lost most of his  notes on Trotzig with those of Papegoja, making it impossible to give  further details.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^His two brothers Anthoni and Johan Trotzig were engaged in cannon  manufacture in Sweden at this time. See Am. Reg., 1650; 1651, June 18; 1652;  June? (F. A.). 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.jstor.org/stable/20084658?seq=1#metadata_info_tab_contents
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/cu31924092229222/cu31924092229222_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Trotzig, Peter, to be employed in place  of Blommaert, 156; buys tobacco in  Holland, 161 ; works for the Com-  pany without pay, 226 ; acts as the  Company's factor in Holland, 233 ;  buys goods for the sixth expedition,  242 ; p
2 CONC urchases brandy and wine for  the tobacco trade, 244; pays return-  ing colonists and soldiers, 248 ; re-  ceives money from Andersson, 253 ;  prepares a ship for New Sweden,  254; orders cloth to be made in  Kampen, 258 ; helps t
2 CONC o prepare the  ninth expedition, 266; aids colonists  returning from the ninth expedition,  276 ; buys large quantities of goods  for a new expedition, 283 ; hires  tobacco spinner, 295 ; Printz writes  to, 338; report made to, 61
2 CONC 6; buys a  ship, 631-632; reports to the Govern-  ment about Finnish families, 650-  651; reports that the English have  captured the colony, 653 ; biography,  697; quoted, 49, 254, 255, 258, 281,  285, 290, 338, 399, 651-652; han
2 CONC d-  writing of, 285-286 
2 CONT
